---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/company.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="About Chudware" description="Why Chudware is a from-scratch parametric CAD system built on the OpenCascade kernel, and how a small team ships it in preview.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>A real CAD system, built from the kernel up.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Chudware is a parametric CAD system for mechanical engineers: constrained sketches, an ordered feature tree, assemblies, CAM, product data, simulation, reverse engineering, and an AI copilot that drives the same modeling tools you do.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-why">
    <div class="wrap co-about-body">
      <div class="co-prose">
        <h2 id="co-why" class="co-anchor">Why build another CAD system</h2>
        <p>Most software that calls itself CAD in a browser is a viewer with a measurement tool bolted on. It can show you a model somebody else made, and it stops there. The moment you need a fully defined sketch, a fillet that fails honestly on a short face, a feature you can reorder, or a toolpath tied to the geometry that produced it, you are back on a workstation with a licence dongle.</p>
        <p>Chudware exists because that gap is an engineering problem, not a marketing one. Solving it means carrying a genuine geometry kernel — <strong>OpenCascade / OCCT 7.9</strong> — all the way through the product, rather than approximating solids with meshes and hoping nobody asks for a section view.</p>

        <h2>What that actually means</h2>
        <p>The system is a large C++, Python, and TypeScript codebase built around the OCCT kernel, a feature tree, a 2D constraint solver, manufacturing workflows, product data, and per-account isolation enforced at the gateway. Design intent stays attached as work moves downstream: the same document carries the sketches, the features, the CAM job, and the project history, instead of being handed between disconnected tools and losing a little truth at every boundary.</p>
        <p>The native file format is <code>.step+</code>, which keeps the feature tree, dimensions, materials, studies, and drawings. STEP and STL exports exist for the moment another tool needs a flattened handoff — but the editable design is not held hostage inside a proprietary blob you cannot open elsewhere.</p>

        <h2>How it is delivered</h2>
        <p>The browser runs the same application as the desktop build, compiled to WebAssembly, rendering with WebGL2 directly in the tab. It talks to the engine over a single JSON-RPC WebSocket, so the geometry kernel and the full engineering toolchain stay intact rather than being reduced to whatever a page can do on its own. Native Windows and Linux preview builds run the same product surface on an engineering workstation and are available on request while a public release channel is prepared. There is no native macOS package today; Macs use the browser workspace.</p>

        <h2>The posture, honestly</h2>
        <p>Chudware is built by a small team and is in <strong>commercial preview</strong>. Preview means the product modes are real and in daily use, and it also means the public rate card is not finished, native distribution is on request, and features still move. Where a page cannot yet state something true, it says so — that is why <a class="text-link" href="/pricing/">pricing</a> publishes no numbers it has not ratified and <a class="text-link" href="/security/">security</a> claims no certification it has not earned.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="co-aside" aria-labelledby="co-posture">
        <svg class="co-mark" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="6.5" y="6.5" width="35" height="35" />
          <path d="M6.5 16.5 H41.5 M16.5 6.5 V41.5" />
          <circle cx="16.5" cy="16.5" r="4.5" />
          <path d="M24 24 L41.5 41.5" />
        </svg>
        <p class="eyebrow" id="co-posture">Engineering posture</p>
        <ol class="co-tenets">
          <li>
            <h3>Kernel first</h3>
            <p>Solids are solids. Features are ordered and editable. Nothing is faked with a mesh to make a demo look finished.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>The architecture is the product</h3>
            <p>The gateway's isolation, the engine bridge, and the file-path boundary are load-bearing engineering, not deployment trivia.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Ship preview builds, label them</h3>
            <p>Users get real releases early and an accurate description of what is unfinished, rather than a polished page over an empty room.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>AI operates the tools</h3>
            <p>The copilot builds normal sketches and features you can inspect and edit. It does not certify a design, and neither does the software.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Say the unflattering part</h3>
            <p>Missing capability, absent audits, and unfinished pricing are stated plainly. It costs a sentence and saves an evaluation.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-notclaimed">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Things this page will not tell you</p>
        <h2 id="co-notclaimed" class="co-anchor">No logo wall, no headcount, no founding legend.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Company pages usually pad with numbers nobody verifies. Chudware would rather leave the space empty than fill it with figures invented for layout. If a customer list, a team size, or a funding line is not published here, it is because it has not been published anywhere.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="co-note" data-lift>
        <p class="eyebrow">What to judge instead</p>
        <p>Look at the product surface: real captures in the <a class="text-link" href="/gallery/">gallery</a>, working documentation in <a class="text-link" href="/docs/">docs</a>, shipped changes in the <a class="text-link" href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, and the enforced controls listed on <a class="text-link" href="/security/">security</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="wrap section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">See for yourself</p>
      <h2>Open the workspace rather than the brochure.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Create an account to use the browser workspace, or ask about a native Windows or Linux preview build.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://app.chudware.com/signup">Create an account</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/gallery/">See the actual product</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
